1
00:00:00,285 --> 00:00:09,542
(Q31) For my project on invertebrates, I chose to study tardigrades. These are microscopic – or to be more precise – near-microscopic animals.

2
00:00:09,542 --> 00:00:17,210
There are well over a thousand known species of these tiny animals, which belong to the phylum Tardigrada.

3
00:00:17,210 --> 00:00:29,362
Most tardigrades range in length from 0.05 to 1 millimetre, though the largest species can grow to be 1.2 millimetres in length.

4
00:00:29,362 --> 00:00:38,238
[Q31答案句] They are also sometimes called 'water bears': 'water' because that's where they thrive best, and 'bear' because of the way they move.

5
00:00:38,238 --> 00:00:45,737
(Q32) 'Moss piglet' is another name for tardigrades because of the way they look when viewed from the front. **“

6
00:00:45,737 --> 00:00:54,968
They were first discovered in Germany in 1773 by Johann Goeze, who coined the name Tardigrada. 1773

7
00:00:54,968 --> 00:01:07,519
As I say, there are many different species of tardigrade – too many to describe here – but, generally speaking, the different species share similar physical traits.

8
00:01:07,519 --> 00:01:16,465
[Q32答案句] They have a body which is short, and also rounded – a bit like a barrel – and the body comprises four segments.

9
00:01:16,465 --> 00:01:23,826
(Q33) Each segment has a pair of legs, at the end of which are between four and eight sharp claws.

10
00:01:23,826 --> 00:01:39,638
[Q33 答案句] I should also say that some species don't have any claws; what they have are discs, and these work by means of suction. They enable the tardigrade to cling on to surfaces or to grip its prey.

11
00:01:39,638 --> 00:01:44,739
(Q34) Within the body, there are no lungs, or any organs for breathing at all.

12
00:01:44,739 --> 00:01:52,891
[Q34答案句] Instead, oxygen and also blood are transported in a fluid that fills the cavity of the body.

13
00:01:52,891 --> 00:02:02,605
(Q35) As far as the tardigrade's head is concerned, the best way I can describe this is that it looks rather strange – a bit squashed even.

14
00:02:02,605 --> 00:02:10,393
Though many of the websites I looked at described its appearance as cute, which isn't exactly very scientific.

15
00:02:10,393 --> 00:02:19,229
[Q35答案句] The tardigrade's mouth is a kind of tube that can open outwards to reveal teeth-like structures known as 'stylets'.

16
00:02:19,229 --> 00:02:23,889
(Q36) These are sharp enough to pierce plant or animal cells.

17
00:02:23,889 --> 00:02:27,329
So, where are tardigrades found?

18
00:02:27,329 --> 00:02:40,181
Well, they live in every part of the world, in a variety of habitats: most commonly, on the bed of a lake, or on many kinds of plants or in very wet environments.

19
00:02:40,181 --> 00:02:49,314
There's been some interesting research which has found that tardigrades are capable of surviving radiation and very high pressure.

20
00:02:49,314 --> 00:03:03,149
[Q36答案句] And they're also able to withstand temperatures as cold as –200 degrees centigrade, or highs of more than 148 degrees centigrade, which is incredibly hot.

21
00:03:03,149 --> 00:03:13,382
(Q37) It has been said that tardigrades could survive long after human beings have been wiped out, even in the event of an asteroid hitting the earth.

22
00:03:13,382 --> 00:03:22,250
If conditions become too extreme and tardigrades are at risk of drying out, they enter a state called cryptobiosis.

23
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:34,502
They curl into a ball, called a tun – that's T-U-N – by retracting their head and legs, and their metabolism drops to less than one percent of normal levels.

24
00:03:34,502 --> 00:03:42,479
They can remain like this until they are re-introduced to water, when they will come back to life in a matter of a few hours.

25
00:03:42,479 --> 00:03:50,454
[Q37答案句] While in a state of cryptobiosis, tardigrades produce a protein that protects their DNA.

26
00:03:50,454 --> 00:03:58,242
(Q38) In 2016, scientists revived two tardigrades that had been tuns for more than 30 years. 2016

27
00:03:58,242 --> 00:04:08,888
There was a report that, in 1948, a 120-year-old tun was revived, but this experiment has never been repeated.

28
00:04:08,888 --> 00:04:21,584
[Q38答案句] There are currently several tests taking place in space, to determine how long tardigrades might be able to survive there. I believe the record so far is 10 days.

29
00:04:21,584 --> 00:04:30,663
(Q39) So, erm, moving on. In terms of their diet, tardigrades consume liquids in order to survive.

30
00:04:30,663 --> 00:04:34,629
Although they have teeth, they don't use these for chewing.

31
00:04:34,629 --> 00:04:46,670
[Q39答案句] They suck the juices from moss, or extract fluid from seaweed, but some species prey on other tardigrades, from other species or within their own.

32
00:04:46,670 --> 00:04:55,367
(Q40) I suppose this isn't surprising, given that tardigrades are mainly comprised of liquid and are coated with a type of gel.

33
00:04:55,367 --> 00:05:00,564
Finally, I'd like to mention the conservation status of tardigrades.

34
00:05:00,564 --> 00:05:11,574
It is estimated that they have been in existence for approximately half a billion years and, in that time, they have survived five mass extinctions.

35
00:05:11,574 --> 00:05:24,653
[Q40答案句] So, it will probably come as no surprise to you, that tardigrades have not been evaluated by the International Union for Conservation of Nature and are not on any endangered list.

36
00:05:24,653 --> 00:05:29,216
Some researchers have described them as thriving.

37
00:05:29,216 --> 00:05:31,716
Does anyone have any questions they'd like to ask?

